<% content_for :title, "Postgres expand contract" %>

<p class="notice"><%= notice %></p>
<p class="alert"><%= alert %></p>

<section class="config">
  <h1>Postgres expand contract</h1>
  <p>A user started with one phone column. <%= term("pgroll", code: false) %> is moving that to many rows in <code>user_phones</code> while the old code and the new code stay up on the same database, so the change has no downtime.</p>
  <p>Both clients use one database. The schema names come from <code>config/pgroll.yml</code>.</p>
  <dl>
    <dt><%= term("old client", code: false) %></dt>
    <dd><%= term(Pgroll.old_search_path) %></dd>
    <dt><%= term("new client", code: false) %></dt>
    <dd><%= term(Pgroll.new_search_path) %></dd>
  </dl>
</section>

<section class="create">
  <h2>Add a user through the <%= term("old client", code: false) %></h2>
  <p>This writes one phone the way the old client does. A leading space stays in the string. The new card opens under this form, and Play replays only that person.</p>
  <%= form_with url: users_path, method: :post do %>
    <label>
      Name
      <input type="text" name="name" required autocomplete="off">
    </label>
    <label>
      Phone
      <input type="text" name="phone" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false">
    </label>
    <button type="submit">Insert user</button>
  <% end %>
</section>

<%= render partial: "person", collection: @people, as: :person %>

<p class="complete"><%= term("pgroll complete", code: false) %> would drop <%= term("public_01_create_users") %>. <%= term("OldUser") %> would then have no schema to use.</p>
